  "worked_solution": "Given that $u$ is orthogonal to $v$ and $w$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v=0 \\\\ u\\cdot w=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the first equation $u\\cdot v=0.$ Calculating the dot product in terms of $a$ and $b$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot 2+3\\cdot (-5)+b\\cdot 3+(-5)\\cdot (-4) & =0 \\\\ 2a-15+3b+20 & =0 \\\\ 2a+3b & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for the second equation $u\\cdot w=0$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot (-4)+3\\cdot (-2)+b\\cdot 5+(-5)\\cdot 3 & =0 \\\\ -4a-6+5b-15 & =0 \\\\ -4a+5b & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the values of $a$ and $b$, we need to solve the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2a+3b=-5 \\\\ -4a+5b=21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $2$ and adding the result to the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n11b=11\\,\u27f9\\,b=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $b=1$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a+3(1) & =-5 \\\\ 2a+3 & =-5 \\\\ 2a & =-8 \\\\ a & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that\n\n$$\na+b=-4+1=-3.\n$$",
